CaumeDSE Sample Clients - Encrypted Secrets and Databases
========================================================

These four clients demonstrate the REST API for encrypted binary secrets,
CSV databases, user metadata and audit logs. This is an application interface,
not a hardware HSM. See each client's README for command and flag details.

  a-web/       Browser application and Python reverse proxy
  b-python/    Python CLI (requires requests)
  c-golang/    Go CLI (Go 1.21 or later; no external modules)
  d-perl/      Perl CLI (LWP::UserAgent, HTTP::Request::Common, URI::Escape;
               HTTPS also requires IO::Socket::SSL)
  env.sh       Sourceable connection defaults; contains no organization key
  cdse_test_manager.py   Isolated four-client DEBUG integration runner

Manual credentials and TLS
--------------------------

The default identifiers are EngineAdmin, EngineOrg and EngineStorage. There
is no universal organization key. Fresh databases generate a random key;
record the first-run console value securely outside CaumeDSE. Existing
databases require their original key. TEST/testDB fixtures have their own
documented key in the repository README.md, not a key supplied by env.sh.

For a manual session, export the actual key without adding it to env.sh:

  read -r -s -p 'Organization key: ' CDSE_ORG_KEY
  export CDSE_ORG_KEY
  source samples/hsm-db-crypto/env.sh

The CLI defaults are https://localhost:8443 and the identifiers above.
All three CLIs honor CDSE_SERVER, CDSE_USER_ID, CDSE_ORG_ID, CDSE_ORG_KEY
and CDSE_STORAGE. Explicit command-line flags override these defaults.
The browser takes credentials in its connection panel instead; proxy.py
takes its upstream URL from --cdse-server or CDSE_SERVER.

HTTPS requires both server verification and valid client authentication.
All CLIs and the proxy support these environment variables:

  CDSE_CA_CERT       PEM CA used to verify the upstream server
  CDSE_CLIENT_CERT   PEM client certificate chain matching the API identity
  CDSE_CLIENT_KEY    PEM unencrypted private key for that chain

Supply both client variables together and protect the private key. Python,
Go and Perl also support --ca-cert / -ca-cert. --insecure / -insecure disables
server verification only; it does not bypass required client authentication.
Use the committed development certificates only for local DEBUG tests.

Quick start (from this directory)
---------------------------------

  python3 b-python/cdse_client.py info

  (cd c-golang && go build -o cdse_client .)
  c-golang/cdse_client info

  perl d-perl/cdse_client.pl info

Start the browser proxy from a-web, with the TLS variables above exported:

  python3 proxy.py --bind 127.0.0.1 --cdse-server https://localhost:8443

Open the displayed browser URL. HTTP is available only for local builds
compiled with --enable-BYPASSTLSAUTHINHTTP. HTTP still requires API credentials.
The manual HTTP proxy port conflict remains tracked in TODO #146; choose a
separate free proxy port when using an HTTP upstream. The test manager chooses
distinct ports automatically.

Common CLI commands
-------------------

  info
  store-secret my_key /path/to/input.bin
  list-secrets
  get-secret my_key /path/to/output.bin
  delete-secret my_key
  db-create contacts id,name,email
  db-insert contacts id=1 name=Alice email=alice@example.com
  db-query contacts
  db-update contacts 1 email=alice@newdomain.com
  db-delete-row contacts 1
  db-list
  audit-log

Use -i / --interactive for prompted commands. Command-line keys are visible
in process listings and shell history; environment variables are also visible
to sufficiently privileged processes. The browser's password input does not
hide credentials in developer tools or network requests.

API mapping
-----------

The document prefix is /organizations/{orgId}/storage/{storage}/documentTypes.
Secrets use file.raw/documents/{name}; CSV databases use file.csv/documents.
Store/create uses POST, listing uses GET, download uses GET on /content,
and deletion uses DELETE. CSV row commands use /contentRows/{N}; updates
use PUT. info uses GET /organizations/{orgId}/users/{userId}, and audit-log
uses GET /transactions. Server-side TLS identity and role authorization
remain required in addition to the organization encryption key.

Isolated automated testing
--------------------------

Build from the repository root:

  ./configure --enable-DEBUG --enable-BYPASSTLSAUTHINHTTP
  make
  python3 samples/hsm-db-crypto/cdse_test_manager.py --help
  python3 samples/hsm-db-crypto/cdse_test_manager.py --protocol both

The default binary is CaumeDSE-debug-tests at the repository root, not
CaumeDSE. --binary /path/to/CaumeDSE-debug-tests or CDSE_BIN can select another
current DEBUG build. The capability check rejects old or release binaries
before creating databases; HTTP also requires the compiled HTTP TLS bypass.
HTTPS-only runs do not require that bypass.

Install requests, Go and the Perl modules listed above before running all
four clients. Certificates are generated with the existing TEST generator,
so openssl and bash must be available. Examples:

  python3 samples/hsm-db-crypto/cdse_test_manager.py --protocol https
  python3 samples/hsm-db-crypto/cdse_test_manager.py --clients python,web
  python3 samples/hsm-db-crypto/cdse_test_manager.py --http-port 18080 --proxy-port 18088

Ports default to automatic free loopback ports. --http-port, --https-port,
--proxy-port and CDSE_DEBUG_TEST_HTTP_PORT / CDSE_DEBUG_TEST_HTTPS_PORT can
select ports explicitly (0 means automatic). --startup-timeout and
--command-timeout bound startup and child/request operations. Unavailable
dependencies fail by default; --allow-missing-clients prints explicit skips,
and an entirely skipped run fails rather than reporting empty success.

Each run creates private temporary databases, payload files and TLS fixtures,
and captures the newly generated key in memory without printing it. Caller
CDSE credentials/configuration, auth-skip flags and HTTP proxies are ignored;
the parent environment and installed databases are not modified. HTTP and
HTTPS start independently with --web-service and --data-dir, with SIGTERM
shutdown, bounded waits and SIGKILL fallback. Interrupts clean up children and
the temporary directory. Do not use SIGKILL on the manager if cleanup matters.

For each protocol, Python, Go and Perl run info, store, list, get and delete;
binary payloads are independently checked against the API and downloaded
files, including absence after deletion. The web check starts the real proxy
and uses browser-equivalent multipart/GET/DELETE requests; it does not automate
the browser JavaScript. HTTPS verifies localhost and authenticates with fresh
client certificates. Readiness requires the runner's real marker and a live
authenticated request; early exits fail promptly. Raw engine/client output is
withheld, including error bodies that may contain credentials or plaintext.

Successful output ends with:

  RESULT clients=4 protocols=2 failed=0

The manager contract tests run under make check; CI additionally runs all
four clients over both protocols. Direct private runner use requires an
existing, owned, non-symlink directory with no group/other access:

  CaumeDSE-debug-tests --web-service http --data-dir PRIVATE_DIRECTORY

This explicit override is DEBUG-only. Normal compiled storage/config/TLS
paths remain unchanged when it is absent; production has no path override.

The manager checks service exit status, including shutdown-time sanitizer
failures. Existing allocation leaks in startup/permission/audit paths are
tracked in TODO #152. Sanitizer CI currently sets ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0;
this enables address/undefined checks but is not a leak-clean claim. A run
with leak detection enabled fails rather than concealing those findings.

Security notes
--------------

Never enable DEBUG authentication bypasses in production. Use HTTPS and
protected key management. Current parameter traces and unauthenticated log
URLs redact credential-style values, but first-run output still prints the
new organization key and authenticated responses may contain sensitive data.
Do not retain raw DEBUG output or assume that encryption of audit records
makes all diagnostic artifacts safe to share. Prefix standard verifier runs
with CDSE_VERIFY_REDACT=1 before sharing retained artifacts.
